1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,270
On 19 January 1983, Apple announced a new personal computer in California,

2
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:17,430
and gave it a woman's name: Lisa.

3
00:00:27,653 --> 00:00:30,523
Sixteen days earlier, on 3 January,

4
00:00:31,093 --> 00:00:33,323
Time magazine had put a computer on its cover,

5
00:00:33,493 --> 00:00:35,323
instead of naming a person of the year.

6
00:00:35,795 --> 00:00:39,310
Inside that issue was a paragraph about the man who ran Apple,

7
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:42,290
and about a baby girl who shared the machine's name.

8
00:00:42,868 --> 00:00:47,518
The girl was Lisa Brennan, and in January 1983 she was four years old.

9
00:00:47,938 --> 00:00:49,843
Steve Jobs was twenty-seven,

10
00:00:49,853 --> 00:00:52,463
and Apple had just finished a year with five hundred

11
00:00:52,473 --> 00:00:54,523
and eighty-three million dollars in sales.

12
00:00:55,195 --> 00:00:58,990
This film follows two Lisas, a computer and a person,

13
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:02,230
and asks what the public record says about each.

14
00:01:02,762 --> 00:01:04,917
It does not guess at anyone's feelings.

15
00:01:05,707 --> 00:01:08,997
It uses a magazine, court reports as others reported them,

16
00:01:09,147 --> 00:01:12,617
a biography, and words Lisa Brennan-Jobs published herself.

17
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:17,130
She is a living author, and her memoir, Small Fry, came out in 2018.

18
00:01:17,867 --> 00:01:20,697
So we take the record as it stands, with its dates,

19
00:01:21,167 --> 00:01:24,917
and we start in 1978, five years before the computer.

20
00:01:25,531 --> 00:01:27,721
Apple had been founded in 1976,

21
00:01:28,231 --> 00:01:31,721
and in 1978 it was a young company selling the Apple Two,

22
00:01:32,091 --> 00:01:35,461
while the machine that would become Lisa was still only a plan.

23
00:01:36,083 --> 00:01:39,948
The story of the girl begins in the same year as the story of the computer,

24
00:01:40,318 --> 00:01:42,148
and the two meet in a magazine.

25
00:01:48,228 --> 00:01:51,323
Lisa was born on 17 May 1978,

26
00:01:51,793 --> 00:01:54,963
on a commune called All One Farm, outside Portland, Oregon.

27
00:01:55,554 --> 00:01:58,844
Her mother was Chrisann Brennan, who had been Jobs's girlfriend,

28
00:01:59,314 --> 00:02:01,704
and Jobs was twenty-three years old.

29
00:02:02,166 --> 00:02:04,761
Time reported that he denied he was the father,

30
00:02:05,271 --> 00:02:07,041
and refused to pay child support.

31
00:02:07,604 --> 00:02:08,294
In her memoir,

32
00:02:08,744 --> 00:02:11,434
Lisa Brennan-Jobs writes that he kept telling people at the farm

33
00:02:11,444 --> 00:02:12,854
that it was not his kid,

34
00:02:13,204 --> 00:02:14,634
though he had come to meet her anyway.

35
00:02:15,245 --> 00:02:18,375
She also writes that her mother supplemented welfare by cleaning houses

36
00:02:18,385 --> 00:02:19,335
and waitressing,

37
00:02:19,785 --> 00:02:23,955
and Isaacson says her mother relied on welfare and food stamps for a time.

38
00:02:24,471 --> 00:02:26,586
This film found no court file online,

39
00:02:26,936 --> 00:02:30,206
so the case is told as Time and the memoir report it.

40
00:02:30,643 --> 00:02:35,518
In 1980 the district attorney of San Mateo County sued Jobs for child support.

41
00:02:46,667 --> 00:02:49,237
A blood test, which Time called voluntary,

42
00:02:49,767 --> 00:02:53,537
put the probability that he was the father at ninety-four point one percent.

43
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:57,450
Jobs answered, in the words Time printed in 1983,

44
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:02,130
that twenty-eight percent of the male population of the United States could be the father.

45
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:05,970
The court ordered him to pay three hundred and eighty-five dollars a month,

46
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:08,810
and to repay the welfare money her mother had received.

47
00:03:09,467 --> 00:03:14,317
On 12 December 1980, Apple went public, and Jobs's shares made him wealthy.

48
00:03:14,912 --> 00:03:16,282
According to Chrisann Brennan,

49
00:03:16,712 --> 00:03:19,822
he raised the payment to five hundred dollars a month after that.

50
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:23,345
Yet two years later, in the pages of a national magazine,

51
00:03:23,575 --> 00:03:24,905
he was still disputing it.

52
00:03:25,293 --> 00:03:29,143
So the record of 1980 is clear on the test and the order,

53
00:03:29,573 --> 00:03:32,303
and clear that Jobs did not accept it in public.

54
00:03:38,428 --> 00:03:40,603
The Time article was written by Jay Cocks,

55
00:03:41,093 --> 00:03:44,163
from reporting by Michael Moritz in Cupertino.

56
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:47,675
Time did not say the computer was named for the girl.

57
00:03:48,245 --> 00:03:51,895
It noted only that the baby and the machine share the same name.

58
00:03:52,411 --> 00:03:56,716
Jobs said afterwards that the article was so awful that he actually cried,

59
00:03:57,146 --> 00:03:58,796
as Fortune reported in 2011.

60
00:03:59,441 --> 00:04:04,431
Apple's explanation, when the machine came out, was an acronym. Lisa,

61
00:04:04,501 --> 00:04:08,291
the company said, stood for Local Integrated Software Architecture.

62
00:04:19,562 --> 00:04:24,037
Computer writers joked that it really stood for, let's invent some acronyms.

63
00:04:24,527 --> 00:04:26,597
Andy Hertzfeld, who worked at Apple,

64
00:04:26,927 --> 00:04:31,017
wrote that the marketing team built the acronym from the name in late 1982,

65
00:04:31,587 --> 00:04:33,777
after the replacement names were all rejected.

66
00:04:34,402 --> 00:04:38,577
Chrisann Brennan says Jobs's first choice for the computer had been Claire,

67
00:04:38,587 --> 00:04:42,517
and that she never gave permission for her daughter's name to be used.

68
00:04:42,981 --> 00:04:45,716
Inside the company, some developers had their own version:

69
00:04:46,086 --> 00:04:48,056
Lisa, invented stupid acronym.

70
00:04:48,613 --> 00:04:50,603
The Lisa did not do what Apple had hoped.

71
00:04:51,573 --> 00:04:55,263
Estimates of how many were sold range from about ten thousand to sixty thousand.

72
00:04:55,872 --> 00:04:58,627
Jobs was taken off the Lisa project before it shipped,

73
00:04:58,997 --> 00:05:00,887
and his attention moved to the Macintosh.

74
00:05:01,633 --> 00:05:04,648
Apple launched it in January and began shipping it in June,

75
00:05:04,658 --> 00:05:07,088
in a campaign that said it works the way you do.

76
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:25,690
In her memoir, Lisa Brennan-Jobs says that as a teenager she asked her father,

77
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:29,410
casually but hopefully, whether the computer was named after her.

78
00:05:29,894 --> 00:05:31,889
She writes that he answered no.

79
00:05:32,444 --> 00:05:35,014
She also writes that she held on to the belief anyway,

80
00:05:35,464 --> 00:05:37,034
and that it helped her sense of herself.

81
00:05:37,533 --> 00:05:41,383
Near the end of his life, Jobs told the biographer Walter Isaacson that,

82
00:05:41,453 --> 00:05:43,683
obviously, it was named for his daughter.

83
00:05:44,205 --> 00:05:47,560
So the record holds two answers to one question: a no,

84
00:05:47,990 --> 00:05:51,920
as she remembers it, and a yes, as Isaacson reported it.

85
00:05:52,307 --> 00:05:54,227
The film does not decide between them.

86
00:05:54,597 --> 00:05:56,927
It sets them side by side, with their dates.

87
00:06:03,028 --> 00:06:06,663
Walter Isaacson's biography says that Jobs later acknowledged her,

88
00:06:06,933 --> 00:06:10,023
and that around the age of fourteen she moved in with him and his family.

89
00:06:10,663 --> 00:06:12,053
According to Chrisann Brennan,

90
00:06:12,403 --> 00:06:14,753
when Lisa was nine she asked to change her surname,

91
00:06:15,043 --> 00:06:18,359
and Jobs had her birth certificate changed to Brennan-Jobs.

92
00:06:18,754 --> 00:06:22,229
In interviews, she has described coldness, and moments of joy.

93
00:06:22,816 --> 00:06:26,366
Jobs told Isaacson that he had done things he was not proud of,

94
00:06:26,516 --> 00:06:29,006
such as getting his girlfriend pregnant at twenty-three,

95
00:06:29,016 --> 00:06:30,146
and the way he handled that.

96
00:06:30,575 --> 00:06:33,985
He also told Isaacson that he wished he had handled it differently,

97
00:06:34,515 --> 00:06:38,425
that he could not see himself as a father then, and so he did not face up to it.

98
00:06:38,831 --> 00:06:43,362
Steve Jobs died on 5 October 2011, at the age of fifty-six.

99
00:06:43,762 --> 00:06:44,992
In October 2011,

100
00:06:45,242 --> 00:06:48,272
CBS's 60 Minutes reported that he had denied paternity

101
00:06:48,282 --> 00:06:51,112
and refused to pay support until the courts intervened.

102
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:53,895
The same report used the word neglected,

103
00:06:54,365 --> 00:06:56,695
and said she moved in with the family as a teenager.

104
00:06:57,390 --> 00:07:00,665
In 2018, Lisa Brennan-Jobs published Small Fry,

105
00:07:00,915 --> 00:07:04,305
a memoir of growing up between her mother's home and her father's.

106
00:07:04,709 --> 00:07:07,159
She has said she wrote it without self-pity,

107
00:07:07,169 --> 00:07:09,719
and chose to start with the parts she was ashamed of.

108
00:07:42,928 --> 00:07:47,643
Set side by side, the record shows a no in 1980, and, decades later,

109
00:07:47,953 --> 00:07:50,903
a father who told a biographer he wished he had done it differently.

110
00:07:57,695 --> 00:08:00,690
Neither the court reports nor the memoir offer a verdict,

111
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:03,130
and this film does not offer one either.

112
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:12,490
The name is still on the computer,

113
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,990
and the child in that magazine paragraph is a writer with a book of her own.

114
00:08:17,617 --> 00:08:22,227
A no in 1980, a yes at the end. Which should the name remember?

